1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:04,032
Last spring, Aziz bought a phone case on the marketplace website.

2
00:00:04,082 --> 00:00:08,376
He typed his number, an SMS code, and a password.

3
00:00:08,426 --> 00:00:13,536
Today, his friend shows him the mobile bank, and Aziz installs it for the first time.

4
00:00:13,586 --> 00:00:23,400
So now one number has two doors: a web account that only an SMS code ever checked, and the mobile bank, which is about to check the SIM card itself.

5
00:00:23,450 --> 00:00:27,000
Should the mobile bank simply trust that old web account?

6
00:00:27,050 --> 00:00:33,432
In Lesson 26, a new customer on the marketplace website got an app account marked not confirmed.

7
00:00:33,482 --> 00:00:38,808
This lesson removes that mark, in the mobile bank, right after the TAF check.

8
00:00:38,858 --> 00:00:40,728
First, why the mark exists.

9
00:00:40,778 --> 00:00:47,352
An SMS code on the web proves one small thing: someone read a message to this number, at that moment.

10
00:00:47,402 --> 00:00:51,504
It says nothing about the phone, or the SIM card, or who was holding it.

11
00:00:51,554 --> 00:00:56,184
A browser cannot run the TAF part, so nothing better was possible there.

12
00:00:56,234 --> 00:00:58,776
Now Aziz types his number in the mobile bank.

13
00:00:58,826 --> 00:01:01,920
The platform sees that this number already has a customer.

14
00:01:01,970 --> 00:01:07,176
So this is not a new registration, and the mobile bank does not make a second customer.

15
00:01:07,226 --> 00:01:10,608
It asks for the password that this number already has.

16
00:01:10,658 --> 00:01:12,480
Aziz types it.

17
00:01:12,530 --> 00:01:19,200
Then TAF sends a one-time code to his number, and checks that it lands on this phone, with this SIM card.

18
00:01:19,250 --> 00:01:21,072
The verdict is green.

19
00:01:21,122 --> 00:01:26,880
Only now, after a correct code and a green verdict, does the platform look at the marketplace.

20
00:01:26,930 --> 00:01:31,824
It finds an app account of this person in the marketplace, still marked not confirmed.

21
00:01:31,874 --> 00:01:34,368
So the mobile bank asks one question.

22
00:01:34,418 --> 00:01:36,936
This number also has a marketplace account.

23
00:01:36,986 --> 00:01:38,808
Is it yours?

24
00:01:38,858 --> 00:01:39,600
Link it?

25
00:01:39,650 --> 00:01:42,768
Aziz remembers the phone case, and he taps Yes.

26
00:01:42,818 --> 00:01:45,576
The marketplace account stays as it is.

27
00:01:45,626 --> 00:01:49,296
Its mark changes to linked, verified through the mobile bank.

28
00:01:49,346 --> 00:01:57,312
From now on, it uses the level of the person, so when Aziz is at level two, the marketplace sees level two too.

29
00:01:57,362 --> 00:01:58,800
That was the easy answer.

30
00:01:58,850 --> 00:02:02,328
Now suppose Aziz had never used the marketplace at all.

31
00:02:02,378 --> 00:02:06,072
Someone else typed his number on the website, long before he did.

32
00:02:06,122 --> 00:02:11,064
That person read one SMS code to his number, and set a password.

33
00:02:11,114 --> 00:02:12,600
So Aziz taps No.

34
00:02:12,650 --> 00:02:15,192
The mobile bank does not act at once.

35
00:02:15,242 --> 00:02:20,808
It asks a second time: This ends all marketplace sessions and closes that account.

36
00:02:20,858 --> 00:02:22,680
Continue?

37
00:02:22,730 --> 00:02:24,000
There is a Back button.

38
00:02:24,050 --> 00:02:27,024
Back changes nothing, and the question stays.

39
00:02:27,074 --> 00:02:28,920
I think this second question is right.

40
00:02:28,970 --> 00:02:35,904
One wrong tap on No would log the real owner out of every marketplace browser, so the app makes him say it twice.

41
00:02:35,954 --> 00:02:37,776
Aziz taps Continue.

42
00:02:37,826 --> 00:02:42,888
The platform ends every marketplace session of this number, on every browser and phone.

43
00:02:42,938 --> 00:02:45,144
The marketplace account is closed.

44
00:02:45,194 --> 00:02:47,016
It is not deleted.

45
00:02:47,066 --> 00:02:52,032
A bank operator can open it again, with a reason, during the retention period.

46
00:02:52,082 --> 00:02:56,760
A later login in the marketplace makes a new app account, on a clean page.

47
00:02:56,810 --> 00:03:00,864
Then the mobile bank asks Aziz to change his password at once.

48
00:03:00,914 --> 00:03:01,608
Why?

49
00:03:01,658 --> 00:03:04,536
Because the person who made the web account may know it.

50
00:03:04,586 --> 00:03:06,408
But look at that again.

51
00:03:06,458 --> 00:03:11,280
If someone else made the web account, Aziz did not know its password in the first place.

52
00:03:11,330 --> 00:03:14,496
So he could not even pass the first step in the mobile bank.

53
00:03:14,546 --> 00:03:16,368
What does he do then?

54
00:03:16,418 --> 00:03:18,984
He resets the password in the mobile bank.

55
00:03:19,034 --> 00:03:26,136
The reset uses the same TAF check, and the mobile bank offers a MyID check in the reset.

56
00:03:26,186 --> 00:03:31,920
Aziz was never at level two, so it is a full MyID check, with his consent.

57
00:03:31,970 --> 00:03:35,952
With a green verdict and MyID, the reset does not wait.

58
00:03:36,002 --> 00:03:39,144
Lesson 30 explains the waiting reset.

59
00:03:39,194 --> 00:03:44,664
And the new master-app session that comes out of the reset outranks every marketplace session.

60
00:03:44,714 --> 00:03:49,080
So the reset itself ends all of the stranger's marketplace sessions.

61
00:03:49,130 --> 00:03:51,552
Lesson 29 explains that rank.

62
00:03:51,602 --> 00:03:57,576
One more rule: each answer writes an audit event and sends an event to the bank's antifraud system.

63
00:03:57,626 --> 00:04:01,056
So the bank can see later who linked what, and who refused.

64
00:04:01,106 --> 00:04:06,336
To recap: an account that only an SMS code checked stays not confirmed.

65
00:04:06,386 --> 00:04:12,192
After a correct code and a green TAF verdict in the mobile bank, the customer answers one question.

66
00:04:12,242 --> 00:04:14,064
Yes links the account.

67
00:04:14,114 --> 00:04:21,768
No, then Continue, ends its sessions and closes it, without deleting it, and the password changes at once.

68
00:04:21,818 --> 00:04:23,640
Aziz said Yes.

69
00:04:23,690 --> 00:04:29,736
His marketplace account is linked, and a week later he passes MyID and is at level two.

70
00:04:29,786 --> 00:04:34,488
Now the marketplace wants to deliver his next order, and it needs his address.

71
00:04:34,538 --> 00:04:37,704
The platform keeps his registered address, from MyID.

72
00:04:37,754 --> 00:04:39,840
Can the marketplace simply read it?

73
00:04:39,890 --> 00:04:41,736
Next: address sharing.
